            <ArticleTitle>An unusual case of painful ophthalmoplegia in a patient with rheumatoid arthritis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>412-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 43-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus had pain and horizontal diplopia. Clinical symptoms were consistent with inflammation of the right superior oblique tendon sheath, but a computed tomographic scan showed no pathology. Treatment with systemic corticosteroids reduced the symptoms, but the patient was left with a persistent muscle imbalance.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
